00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:38.460
anybody knows what that is?

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:44.300
Swamp, correct.

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:46.430
And the swamp here
pictured is very

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:48.740
expressing to some
of the challenges

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:50.510
that we're seeing
with our customers.

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:52.890
People started looking
into Hadoop, probably

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:57.110
around 2008, 2009,
and started migrating

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:59.600
some of their existing
data to Hadoop.

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:02.660
And lots of hype happened
when it comes to Hadoop.

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:06.330
And a lot of customers
ended up with lots of data

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:08.060
being migrated to
Hadoop, whether that's

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:10.550
needed or not needed, the
right solution or not,

00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:11.300
it doesn't matter.

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:14.660
In IT, we look for these
type of new technologies,

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:16.250
and we start hyping them.

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:19.520
And suddenly, we think that
is the right answer for many

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:21.450
of the problems that we have.

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:25.340
So a data lake that's supposed
to be nice, clear, pristine,

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:27.560
that allows you
to get and gather

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:31.640
the right insights from the
data that you have turns out

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:34.580
to be a data swamp,
because of the fact

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:37.400
that the insight that was
promised when the data was

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:40.550
started to be migrated
is not being fulfilled,

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:45.410
along with the fact that the
performance and the issues

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:47.330
around maintenance
and, the expenses

00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:48.950
that are associated
with data lakes

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:50.930
are spiraling out of control.

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:55.760
And that basically
puts a bucket of water,

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:57.900
if you will, cold water
on the whole process.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:01.550
And people start
becoming less trusting

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:03.740
of these type of
hype-type of technologies,

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:05.940
and it impacts the
whole organization.

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:11.000
So this diagram basically
shows a heart, not necessarily

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:14.030
a broken one, in case you
think that it is the case.

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:15.020
It's just a heart.

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:18.079
And that heart represents the
vitality of your organization.

00:04:18.079 --> 00:04:21.470
That vitality basically
comes from a variety

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:22.610
of type of factors.

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:24.080
You can look at
your infrastructure

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:25.880
as one factor of that vitality.

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:27.932
You can look at
your platforms that

00:04:27.932 --> 00:04:29.640
are based on that type
of infrastructure,

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:32.630
and you can look at your
applications that are basically

00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:34.310
being built on top of that.

00:04:34.310 --> 00:04:38.617
What we're seeing being built
today, these type of things,

00:04:38.617 --> 00:04:39.200
they're great.

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:42.849
We can do a great job on
these type of systems.

00:04:42.849 --> 00:04:44.390
But we're seeing
missing in the field

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:47.990
that the data is the
missing link when it comes

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:49.280
to these types of systems.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:50.490
We have data.

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:53.690
But we don't have the right
set-up, the right quality.

00:04:53.690 --> 00:04:56.570
We cannot get the right
insights out of that data.

00:04:56.570 --> 00:04:59.344
So that becomes a challenge,
and that basically

00:04:59.344 --> 00:05:01.010
contributes to the
fact that you are not

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:03.590
realizing the full
potential of the data

00:05:03.590 --> 00:05:05.420
as part of the process.

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:08.330
Now, some of these
challenges that we

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:09.950
have when it comes to data--

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:12.680
and I think probably all
of you would relate to it--

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:13.730
data silos.

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:18.200
Anybody who has never
encountered any type of data

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:22.670
silos within the organization,
I would love to meet them.

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:26.990
So data silos is a common
problem, and even with Hadoop.

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:29.990
When people started
building data lakes,

00:05:29.990 --> 00:05:32.120
the premise was you
have a data lake

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:35.060
so that you dump all the
data in one single lake.

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:37.400
Then when you go and
talk to customers,

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:41.210
you start looking at somebody
has Cloudera somewhere.

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:42.770
They have MapR somewhere.

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:46.250
They have other type of
distributions in data lakes.

00:05:46.250 --> 00:05:48.770
And the question that
basically needs to be asked,

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:50.630
why is that happening?

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:52.910
And that's part of
the other symptom

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:55.460
that we're having, because
of the fact that the data

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:58.160
is growing at
significant type of rate.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:05:59.540
And that significant
type of rate

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:03.420
basically is forcing you
to look at it and say,

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:05.450
well, there has to be
something good out of it,

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:07.130
and I need to
store it somewhere.

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:09.050
And you start adding
and adding and adding.

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:11.660
And that becomes a
problem in itself.

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:13.730
And the questions
that should have

00:06:13.730 --> 00:06:17.300
been asked when you start adding
this data type of attributes

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:20.660
and these data volumes should
have been asked in terms of,

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:22.970
well, is that the right
data that I need to look at?

00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:24.830
Is it in the right format?

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:26.810
Are we going to use that data?

00:06:26.810 --> 00:06:29.570
Do I need to go and store
it in its existing format?

00:06:29.570 --> 00:06:31.580
Or do I need to
manipulate it before I go

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:33.440
and get it into my data lake.

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:35.870
These questions, in
many of our customers

00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:38.180
where they have these
type of situations,

00:06:38.180 --> 00:06:39.530
are not being asked.

00:06:39.530 --> 00:06:41.390
And that's part of
the problem that's

00:06:41.390 --> 00:06:44.750
being manifested by the fact
that I have a data lake,

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:47.000
but I'm not fulfilling the
promise of that data lake.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:49.010
And can you please
come and help us look

00:06:49.010 --> 00:06:51.334
into what we can do about it.

00:06:51.334 --> 00:06:53.000
So these are a type
of common challenges

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:54.770
that we see with our customers.

00:06:54.770 --> 00:06:57.320
And many times,
when customers start

00:06:57.320 --> 00:07:00.530
with these type of things,
they start looking into a POC.

00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:02.750
In a POC type of setting,
everything is great.

00:07:02.750 --> 00:07:04.250
You take a simple use case.

00:07:04.250 --> 00:07:06.290
You create a POC.

00:07:06.290 --> 00:07:09.370
You run successfully
with it, and you say, OK,

00:07:09.370 --> 00:07:10.740
well let's continue with that.

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:12.080
That's not the right answer.

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:16.520
A POC is a point in time, small
snapshot that basically needs

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:17.960
to be taken with
a grain of salt.

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:20.000
When you're done
with that POC, there

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:23.330
has to be some type
of expansion into what

00:07:23.330 --> 00:07:25.320
does it mean when it
goes into production,

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:28.370
and what type of extra
additional steps or corners

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:32.090
that you have cut in order to
make a timeline for that POC,

00:07:32.090 --> 00:07:35.450
and achieve that success
criteria that you agreed upon

00:07:35.450 --> 00:07:37.950
before you started that POC.

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:40.430
Oftentimes, you
constrict the scope

00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:44.120
so that you can do this
in four weeks, six weeks.

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:45.650
Everybody feels
excited about it.

00:07:45.650 --> 00:07:47.870
Then that continues to be
part of the organization.

00:07:47.870 --> 00:07:50.810
And then that's where the
problem starts spiraling up.

00:07:50.810 --> 00:07:56.450
And then the fact that we
have lots and lots of data

00:07:56.450 --> 00:07:59.990
explosions, and the fact that
if you look at the database

00:07:59.990 --> 00:08:02.780
vendors five, seven
years ago, there

00:08:02.780 --> 00:08:04.730
used to be the typical
four or five vendors.

00:08:04.730 --> 00:08:08.990
And it was a very easy
selection between A and B and C

00:08:08.990 --> 00:08:10.010
companies.

00:08:10.010 --> 00:08:12.380
Now if you look at the
diagram, you'll probably

00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:16.190
see 50, 60, 70 type of vendors.

00:08:16.190 --> 00:08:21.170
Everybody is vying for the
best in XYZ different type

00:08:21.170 --> 00:08:23.210
of structures coming up.

00:08:23.210 --> 00:08:26.420
Lots of companies are basically
having issues and doubts

00:08:26.420 --> 00:08:30.050
understanding what does it mean
to be in this type of format

00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:31.820
or that type of format.

00:08:31.820 --> 00:08:34.140
So that's part of what's
contributing to the mix,

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:39.169
as well, and the fact that we
have lots of skills issues that

00:08:39.169 --> 00:08:42.620
are hard, basically, for
everybody to keep up with,

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:46.040
because the change of the
technology is so significant.

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:48.610
And I'm sure many of
you in this session

00:08:48.610 --> 00:08:49.610
are suffering from that.

00:08:49.610 --> 00:08:51.290
You just can't keep up.

00:08:51.290 --> 00:08:54.320
You have to be aware
of lots of things

00:08:54.320 --> 00:08:56.340
in order to keep up
with the pace of change.

00:08:56.340 --> 00:08:59.240
And if that's part
of your situation,

00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:01.820
then it becomes a problem
for the organization where

00:09:01.820 --> 00:09:04.640
you don't have the right skills
to make the right decisions.

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:06.920
And compound that
with additional type

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:10.790
of different, complex
type of platforms where

00:09:10.790 --> 00:09:12.950
you need to be in
different type of settings

00:09:12.950 --> 00:09:14.660
with hybrid different platforms.

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:16.320
That makes it even harder.

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:18.440
So these are some
of the challenges

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:20.240
that we're seeing in the field.

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:23.780
And to look at these
challenges, and not

00:09:23.780 --> 00:09:26.210
to do anything
about them would be

00:09:26.210 --> 00:09:30.440
something that would set you up
for even failure down the road.

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:33.260
Now what's our approach
for data migration

00:09:33.260 --> 00:09:35.100
in Accenture or general?

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:37.550
And before I go and address
the data migration approach,

00:09:37.550 --> 00:09:40.510
let me just talk
about three factors,

00:09:40.510 --> 00:09:44.270
or reminders to remind
ourselves before we

00:09:44.270 --> 00:09:45.600
get into the approach.

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:48.320
The first one is, remember
always the objective.

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:52.329
State the objective, and try to
measure against that objective.

00:09:52.329 --> 00:09:53.870
That will help you
down the road when

00:09:53.870 --> 00:09:56.990
you assess whether what you
have done is correct or wrong.

00:09:56.990 --> 00:10:00.090
If your objective was I
am building this project,

00:10:00.090 --> 00:10:01.910
whether it's Hadoop
infrastructure

00:10:01.910 --> 00:10:05.150
on-prem or on-cloud or
machine learning, you name it.

00:10:05.150 --> 00:10:07.450
My objective is
ABC, and here's how

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:09.270
I'm going to measure
that objective.

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:11.880
And here are the metrics that
I'm going to measure about.

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:15.130
Because if you don't put
these type of things,

00:10:15.130 --> 00:10:17.450
there is no guarantee
that when you finish

00:10:17.450 --> 00:10:20.180
at the end of the day when,
the CIO or the CTO comes to you

00:10:20.180 --> 00:10:23.060
and says, how are we
doing on this, well,

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:25.730
we didn't set an objective,
therefore everything will go.

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:27.704
But if you set
certain objectives,

00:10:27.704 --> 00:10:29.870
you have to report against
these type of objectives.

00:10:29.870 --> 00:10:32.260
And it keeps you honest as
well as part of the process.

00:10:32.260 --> 00:10:34.340
So it's part of
the self-governance

00:10:34.340 --> 00:10:36.800
that you need to instill as
part of the organization,

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:38.570
and starts with that.

00:10:38.570 --> 00:10:40.790
And as part of the
process, remember

00:10:40.790 --> 00:10:44.840
that we want to derive
value out of data

00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:46.670
to enable ubiquitous
type of intelligence,

00:10:46.670 --> 00:10:48.365
what we call in Accenture.

00:10:48.365 --> 00:10:50.240
Different companies call
it different things.

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:52.820
But we want to have that type
of intelligence, insights

00:10:52.820 --> 00:10:54.640
that we want to
bring to the table.

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:56.360
And we want to make
sure that we are

00:10:56.360 --> 00:10:58.310
bound by these type of
objectives and goals

00:10:58.310 --> 00:10:59.940
as part of the process.

00:10:59.940 --> 00:11:01.850
And as you go and
try to accomplish

00:11:01.850 --> 00:11:04.466
that type of objective,
which is a very noble one,

00:11:04.466 --> 00:11:06.090
there are different
types of companies.

00:11:06.090 --> 00:11:08.257
We know that different
type of customers

00:11:08.257 --> 00:11:09.590
have different type of settings.

00:11:09.590 --> 00:11:11.690
And what's appropriate
for a small business

00:11:11.690 --> 00:11:14.930
may not be appropriate
for a large enterprise.

00:11:14.930 --> 00:11:19.040
So you would have to take
that with my company, five

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:22.820
people company, 10 people,
or 100, or 1,000, or 20,000,

00:11:22.820 --> 00:11:26.690
with 2,000 people in IT--

00:11:26.690 --> 00:11:29.660
different skills
mean different things

00:11:29.660 --> 00:11:32.750
how you can accomplish or go
about what type of approach

00:11:32.750 --> 00:11:37.920
that you want to take when
it comes to data migration.

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:41.360
And if you were to look at
it, the way we look at it

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:45.230
at Accenture, we have a
maturity scale, if you will,

00:11:45.230 --> 00:11:46.640
where it's incremental.

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:50.660
So customers look at it
and start looking at, well,

00:11:50.660 --> 00:11:53.690
one of the first things that
we do, we look at data storage

00:11:53.690 --> 00:11:55.250
type of migrating to the cloud.

00:11:55.250 --> 00:11:57.020
That's one of the
easiest things that

00:11:57.020 --> 00:11:59.744
will get your feet wet as
part of the process, where

00:11:59.744 --> 00:12:01.910
you can start migrating
some of these type of things

00:12:01.910 --> 00:12:02.900
to the cloud.

00:12:02.900 --> 00:12:04.700
As you start getting
them into the cloud,

00:12:04.700 --> 00:12:06.860
you start looking at
your applications.

00:12:06.860 --> 00:12:09.590
What can I do within my
application type of domain

00:12:09.590 --> 00:12:12.830
so that I can leverage
this data that I have just

00:12:12.830 --> 00:12:14.450
migrated to the cloud?

00:12:14.450 --> 00:12:16.160
As you start looking
into that, you

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:19.760
start looking for ways to
optimize your data architecture

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:21.950
for new, different
type of applications

00:12:21.950 --> 00:12:25.724
that you basically say,
I no longer like the fact

00:12:25.724 --> 00:12:27.890
that we're bound by these
type of legacy constraints

00:12:27.890 --> 00:12:28.920
that we had earlier.

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:31.520
So we're trying to
re-architect systems

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:33.671
that leverage
everything on the cloud.

00:12:33.671 --> 00:12:35.420
That's what we call
born-on-the-cloud type

00:12:35.420 --> 00:12:38.060
of things, or native type
of cloud applications.

00:12:38.060 --> 00:12:42.230
And as you start gaining the
lessons learned and experience,

00:12:42.230 --> 00:12:44.880
our objective is, at
the end of the day--

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:47.870
and it's an incremental one,
just let me emphasize that--

00:12:47.870 --> 00:12:49.430
is to get to what
we call the "data

00:12:49.430 --> 00:12:51.740
intelligence" on the
cloud, whether that

00:12:51.740 --> 00:12:55.140
means machine learning, AI, or
whatever it is, you name it.

00:12:55.140 --> 00:12:56.780
It doesn't matter.

00:12:56.780 --> 00:13:00.950
I want to extract that
insight from the cloud

00:13:00.950 --> 00:13:02.720
so that I have the
right consumers,

00:13:02.720 --> 00:13:06.110
I have the right insights
at my fingertips.

00:13:06.110 --> 00:13:08.510
And that would allow me
to impact the business,

00:13:08.510 --> 00:13:12.400
so that I can operate using
new, different business models,

00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:13.310
new revenue streams.

00:13:13.310 --> 00:13:17.630
Because I'm able to consider
new patterns that my data is

00:13:17.630 --> 00:13:18.470
allowing me to do.

00:13:18.470 --> 00:13:21.170
And that intelligence is
giving me insight such

00:13:21.170 --> 00:13:23.090
that I can go and
start marketing

00:13:23.090 --> 00:13:24.800
to a different segment
that I completely

00:13:24.800 --> 00:13:27.215
ignored before using that
type of intelligence,

00:13:27.215 --> 00:13:29.480
or before using
machine learning.

00:13:29.480 --> 00:13:31.430
New different
clusters of customers

00:13:31.430 --> 00:13:36.050
are coming up, a new way of
discovering new medicines

00:13:36.050 --> 00:13:38.630
or interactions between
different medical type

00:13:38.630 --> 00:13:41.390
of components.

00:13:41.390 --> 00:13:43.430
So all of these type of
things, the objective

00:13:43.430 --> 00:13:46.220
is to get to that data
supply chain on the cloud

00:13:46.220 --> 00:13:51.650
so that we re-architect
our on-prem type of data

00:13:51.650 --> 00:13:54.470
so that it's modernized
and extended to the cloud.

00:13:54.470 --> 00:13:56.330
And at the end of
the day, I can create

00:13:56.330 --> 00:14:00.470
these type of services that
would allow me to go there.

00:14:00.470 --> 00:14:02.810
And as part of that
process, basically, you

00:14:02.810 --> 00:14:06.380
have to go and look at what
can I do differently to get

00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:08.900
to that level of maturity.

00:14:08.900 --> 00:14:11.024
So it all starts with capturing.

00:14:11.024 --> 00:14:13.190
So we have different type
of formats, different type

00:14:13.190 --> 00:14:15.110
of data that's
coming along the way

00:14:15.110 --> 00:14:17.060
from different type of sources.

00:14:17.060 --> 00:14:19.670
Is your existing
type of practices

00:14:19.670 --> 00:14:22.592
capable of capturing
that type of data?

00:14:22.592 --> 00:14:24.050
And most likely,
the answer is not.

00:14:24.050 --> 00:14:25.730
So you have to go
out and look at that,

00:14:25.730 --> 00:14:28.490
and assess what's needed
in order to get there.

00:14:28.490 --> 00:14:30.580
Once you capture, curate.

00:14:30.580 --> 00:14:33.710
Put that with 10
underlines there,

00:14:33.710 --> 00:14:37.440
because that's one of the
premises that would guarantee

00:14:37.440 --> 00:14:39.500
you that you will not
fall into that data

00:14:39.500 --> 00:14:41.190
swamp type of practice.

00:14:41.190 --> 00:14:43.430
It's one of them,
but it's the key one.

00:14:43.430 --> 00:14:45.170
When you start curating
your data, that

00:14:45.170 --> 00:14:48.470
means basically not a single
person will come and say,

00:14:48.470 --> 00:14:51.007
we'll just get that file and
dump it into the data lake.

00:14:51.007 --> 00:14:53.090
There would have to be
lots of checks and balances

00:14:53.090 --> 00:14:55.910
that basically would say,
why are you doing that?

00:14:55.910 --> 00:14:57.800
What type of data that
we're getting into?

00:14:57.800 --> 00:15:00.030
What type of data profiling
that needs to be applied?

00:15:00.030 --> 00:15:02.090
What type of metadata,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:15:02.090 --> 00:15:03.200
That needs to get there.

00:15:03.200 --> 00:15:05.960
And once you do
that, I can enable

00:15:05.960 --> 00:15:09.770
the consumption of that data
in my type of organization.

00:15:09.770 --> 00:15:11.750
And then I enable
additional things

00:15:11.750 --> 00:15:13.070
related to manage and govern.

00:15:13.070 --> 00:15:15.620
Because once you get it
there, you need to manage it.

00:15:15.620 --> 00:15:17.390
You need to govern it.

00:15:17.390 --> 00:15:19.610
And that becomes a
continuous cycle.

00:15:19.610 --> 00:15:22.700
And then as new things
happen, you need to evolve.

00:15:22.700 --> 00:15:24.562
You don't just do
it once and say,

00:15:24.562 --> 00:15:26.270
I'm done with it,
because there is always

00:15:26.270 --> 00:15:28.310
a new data format
that would come up,

00:15:28.310 --> 00:15:31.850
that may expedite things,
that may do 10x load

00:15:31.850 --> 00:15:33.830
factor for your data,
that could be something

00:15:33.830 --> 00:15:36.050
that you want to look into.

00:15:36.050 --> 00:15:38.630
And then how you can
operate that data,

00:15:38.630 --> 00:15:41.700
and get it into the new
ways of doing things,

00:15:41.700 --> 00:15:43.430
whether that's
social, mobile, IoT,

00:15:43.430 --> 00:15:47.330
whatever it is that we hear
about all this time as part

00:15:47.330 --> 00:15:53.610
of the evolving of technology
that we see on a daily basis.

00:15:53.610 --> 00:15:56.660
Now, this slide is not
intended to scare you,

00:15:56.660 --> 00:16:00.740
especially for those who are
on the small and medium size.

00:16:00.740 --> 00:16:03.260
This slide is
really here to show

00:16:03.260 --> 00:16:06.800
the level of sophistication
and complexity

00:16:06.800 --> 00:16:10.610
that is required in order to
get to the right data practices.

00:16:10.610 --> 00:16:11.210
Why is that?

00:16:11.210 --> 00:16:16.010
Because it's not an Accenture
invention if you look at it.

00:16:16.010 --> 00:16:19.430
I mean, it starts all the
way from vision, and mission,

00:16:19.430 --> 00:16:22.430
and creating the right data
strategy for your organization,

00:16:22.430 --> 00:16:25.400
all the way down to what
type of measurements,

00:16:25.400 --> 00:16:28.010
and then the cross-cutting
concerns around governance

00:16:28.010 --> 00:16:29.210
management.

00:16:29.210 --> 00:16:33.130
If you are an SI sitting in this
room, you come and say, well,

00:16:33.130 --> 00:16:35.060
we have something similar.

00:16:35.060 --> 00:16:37.400
And I'm sure a lot of you
are doing something similar

00:16:37.400 --> 00:16:38.120
to that.

00:16:38.120 --> 00:16:42.620
And I raise it here just
because it's needed.

00:16:42.620 --> 00:16:45.380
Whether you implement
100% of that

00:16:45.380 --> 00:16:47.540
or you implement 50%
of that, or whatever

00:16:47.540 --> 00:16:50.360
percentage that you think is
appropriate for your business,

00:16:50.360 --> 00:16:52.610
remember the three principles
we talked about earlier.

00:16:52.610 --> 00:16:53.940
I have an objective.

00:16:53.940 --> 00:16:55.550
I have a different
way to approach it,

00:16:55.550 --> 00:16:58.730
whether I'm small and medium
versus a large organization.

00:16:58.730 --> 00:17:01.550
And similarly, I look at
this type of methodology

00:17:01.550 --> 00:17:04.500
and say, what do I need
to take out of that?

00:17:04.500 --> 00:17:07.190
And that becomes a decision
point for your organization,

00:17:07.190 --> 00:17:10.400
so that you look at it, and
say, for my organization,

00:17:10.400 --> 00:17:12.920
I'm going to do these
type of activities,

00:17:12.920 --> 00:17:17.150
or I'm going to do some of them,
and I'm skipping X, Y, and Z.

00:17:17.150 --> 00:17:18.560
And you document that.

00:17:18.560 --> 00:17:20.329
And there should be
a good, valid reason

00:17:20.329 --> 00:17:21.890
why aren't you doing that.

00:17:21.890 --> 00:17:25.020
So that becomes an important
thing so that you are aware,

00:17:25.020 --> 00:17:28.250
at least you give it a thought,
for my type of situation,

00:17:28.250 --> 00:17:29.720
I don't need that.

00:17:29.720 --> 00:17:32.270
And here is looking at
it from an agnostic type

00:17:32.270 --> 00:17:33.140
of perspective.

00:17:33.140 --> 00:17:35.630
You start looking at your
data sources, data staging,

00:17:35.630 --> 00:17:38.930
data ingestion, how you are
going to curate the data.

00:17:38.930 --> 00:17:41.810
All of these type of
things are decision points.

00:17:41.810 --> 00:17:45.380
What are you doing about that
type of real-time ingestion

00:17:45.380 --> 00:17:47.120
for this type of data formats?

00:17:47.120 --> 00:17:50.090
Are you having to deal
with that type of setup,

00:17:50.090 --> 00:17:52.620
or is that something
that's not necessary?

00:17:52.620 --> 00:17:54.770
And if you do have
this type of thing

00:17:54.770 --> 00:17:56.690
and you're not doing
anything about it,

00:17:56.690 --> 00:17:59.420
are you losing any type
of benefits as a business

00:17:59.420 --> 00:18:01.940
from not paying enough
attention to that type of data?

00:18:04.620 --> 00:18:06.677
So let me switch gears
to data migration to GCP.

00:18:06.677 --> 00:18:08.510
And I think that's where
the majority of you

00:18:08.510 --> 00:18:11.190
are coming here as
part of the process.

00:18:11.190 --> 00:18:12.787
So we covered the methodology.

00:18:12.787 --> 00:18:14.370
And the key point
from the methodology

00:18:14.370 --> 00:18:18.660
is the fact that it's not
a trivial type of endeavor.

00:18:18.660 --> 00:18:20.840
If you talk to
somebody, and I have

00:18:20.840 --> 00:18:24.230
talked to many customers in the
past, where they come to us--

00:18:24.230 --> 00:18:26.490
we'll just move
that data from here

00:18:26.490 --> 00:18:29.560
to Google Cloud in 10
minutes or one hour.

00:18:29.560 --> 00:18:31.610
That's it.

00:18:31.610 --> 00:18:33.350
That is the wrong answer.

00:18:33.350 --> 00:18:34.910
Yes, we can do that.

00:18:34.910 --> 00:18:36.470
But is that what we want?

00:18:36.470 --> 00:18:38.000
The answer is no.

00:18:38.000 --> 00:18:41.120
And for the previous
reasons, if that

00:18:41.120 --> 00:18:45.050
has planted the seeds of doubt,
when somebody comes talks

00:18:45.050 --> 00:18:48.651
to you about this
type of proposition,

00:18:48.651 --> 00:18:50.900
I think you need to be asking
these types of questions

00:18:50.900 --> 00:18:52.490
when somebody
comes and says it's

00:18:52.490 --> 00:18:54.800
an hour type of job, or
two hours, or three days,

00:18:54.800 --> 00:18:56.000
or whatever it is.

00:18:56.000 --> 00:18:59.300
Because the objective is not
to get the data to the cloud.

00:18:59.300 --> 00:19:01.850
The objective is what can
I do with that data, what

00:19:01.850 --> 00:19:04.040
type of insight, what
type of governance

00:19:04.040 --> 00:19:05.690
that needs to be there.

00:19:05.690 --> 00:19:08.900
So let's keep that in mind as
we look into data migration

00:19:08.900 --> 00:19:11.570
to GCP, because I'm going
to be claiming that it's

00:19:11.570 --> 00:19:13.580
going to take 10 minutes.

00:19:13.580 --> 00:19:15.200
Because yes, it does.

00:19:15.200 --> 00:19:18.440
But keep that in mind.

00:19:18.440 --> 00:19:21.830
So the first question when
it comes to data to GCP

00:19:21.830 --> 00:19:25.460
is the question around
speed of network

00:19:25.460 --> 00:19:28.770
and how much data do you
have as part of the process.

00:19:28.770 --> 00:19:33.380
So if you're trying to migrate
10 terabytes or 100 terabytes

00:19:33.380 --> 00:19:36.890
of data, and you're
having a network

00:19:36.890 --> 00:19:39.980
speed of 100
megabits per second,

00:19:39.980 --> 00:19:41.720
you can see that
for 10 terabytes,

00:19:41.720 --> 00:19:43.280
I'm going to take 12 days.

00:19:43.280 --> 00:19:47.330
For 10 megabits I'm
going to take 124 days.

00:19:47.330 --> 00:19:50.090
If you happen to be on the
lucky side of 1 gigabyte, or 10

00:19:50.090 --> 00:19:52.900
gigabyte, or 100, even, it's
going to take much less.

00:19:52.900 --> 00:19:55.025
So the question you will
basically need to ask

00:19:55.025 --> 00:19:58.310
is, am I going to do this
on a physical type of manner

00:19:58.310 --> 00:19:59.570
versus online?

00:19:59.570 --> 00:20:01.370
And you use this
chart, basically,

00:20:01.370 --> 00:20:02.900
to help you to decide.

00:20:02.900 --> 00:20:06.200
If you all happen to be
on this lower corner here,

00:20:06.200 --> 00:20:09.740
most likely, you are going to
use a physical type of approach

00:20:09.740 --> 00:20:15.350
to move your data from whatever
location into Google Cloud.

00:20:15.350 --> 00:20:19.130
Next question that
comes to be asked,

00:20:19.130 --> 00:20:21.200
and it's an important
question, is

00:20:21.200 --> 00:20:23.300
if you were to look at
the Google data constructs

00:20:23.300 --> 00:20:26.670
that we have out of GCP, if you
look at it for the first time,

00:20:26.670 --> 00:20:28.555
it could be overwhelming.

00:20:28.555 --> 00:20:30.680
So the next question that
basically you need to ask

00:20:30.680 --> 00:20:34.550
is what type of data
I'm trying to migrate.

00:20:34.550 --> 00:20:36.740
And if you start from
top, you can look at it

00:20:36.740 --> 00:20:38.840
from two different
classifications.

00:20:38.840 --> 00:20:41.470
Either it's structured
data or unstructured data.

00:20:41.470 --> 00:20:44.960
If it's unstructured data,
it's pretty much easy.

00:20:44.960 --> 00:20:47.390
If I don't have a need
for mobile as the case,

00:20:47.390 --> 00:20:49.430
Google Cloud
Storage most likely,

00:20:49.430 --> 00:20:53.030
an object type of storage,
will be the answer to you.

00:20:53.030 --> 00:20:55.750
If you were to say, it's
more of a structured data,

00:20:55.750 --> 00:20:58.520
than we look at it
from OLTP versus all

00:20:58.520 --> 00:21:00.850
that type of classification.

00:21:00.850 --> 00:21:03.110
If it has more of a
transactional load,

00:21:03.110 --> 00:21:05.450
we have a decision point
that we need to make.

00:21:05.450 --> 00:21:08.090
If it has more of
analytics type of load,

00:21:08.090 --> 00:21:10.880
we have the Bigtable
versus BigQuery.

00:21:10.880 --> 00:21:13.190
And the primary difference
is in the type of latency

00:21:13.190 --> 00:21:17.510
that you get in terms of
Bigtable versus BigQuery.

00:21:17.510 --> 00:21:20.330
If it's on the transactional
workload, then you look at it

00:21:20.330 --> 00:21:23.000
and say, do I have a
preference for SQL type

00:21:23.000 --> 00:21:25.250
of interfaces versus no SQL?

00:21:25.250 --> 00:21:28.940
And that's where you look at
that and say, for SQL type,

00:21:28.940 --> 00:21:33.110
we have the cloud SQL
with MySQL and Postgres

00:21:33.110 --> 00:21:36.350
as the two flavors that you
get out of the box in GCP,

00:21:36.350 --> 00:21:39.110
plus Cloud Data Spanner for
the horizontal scalability

00:21:39.110 --> 00:21:41.840
and real-time type of
applications that require

00:21:41.840 --> 00:21:43.700
that level of performance.

00:21:43.700 --> 00:21:47.390
And for those who want to do
the newest SQL document type

00:21:47.390 --> 00:21:50.390
of story, you have Cloud
Datastore and Firebase Realtime

00:21:50.390 --> 00:21:54.801
DB, especially for needing
mobile SDKs you can use that,

00:21:54.801 --> 00:21:57.050
but doesn't necessarily mean
that you cannot use Cloud

00:21:57.050 --> 00:21:58.582
Datastore with mobile devices.

00:21:58.582 --> 00:22:00.290
It just gives you
additional capabilities

00:22:00.290 --> 00:22:03.020
when it comes to notifications
and these type of things,

00:22:03.020 --> 00:22:06.020
that makes life a little
bit easier for you.

00:22:06.020 --> 00:22:08.000
The third question
that you need to ask

00:22:08.000 --> 00:22:11.950
is, I figured out it's going
to be physical or online.

00:22:11.950 --> 00:22:15.200
I figured out what type of
construct that I need to get.

00:22:15.200 --> 00:22:19.952
Then the third question is,
how can I get my data into GCP?

00:22:19.952 --> 00:22:22.160
And that's what this slide
is trying to address here.

00:22:22.160 --> 00:22:24.860
And look at it in
terms of distance

00:22:24.860 --> 00:22:27.080
to GCP at the
bottom, the x-axis.

00:22:27.080 --> 00:22:29.210
And the y-axis is
the amount of data.

00:22:29.210 --> 00:22:32.030
So from x-axis perspective,
you look at it,

00:22:32.030 --> 00:22:34.610
if you are GCS, or
Google Cloud Storage,

00:22:34.610 --> 00:22:36.560
the data is already
there on Google Cloud.

00:22:36.560 --> 00:22:37.851
Could be in a different region.

00:22:37.851 --> 00:22:39.710
You want to shift it
to another region.

00:22:39.710 --> 00:22:43.880
Or it could be in S3 Buckets,
Amazon, or something else.

00:22:43.880 --> 00:22:45.920
Or it could be in a
colo type of facility

00:22:45.920 --> 00:22:49.189
with good type of
connectivity, or on-prem

00:22:49.189 --> 00:22:50.480
with that type of connectivity.

00:22:50.480 --> 00:22:53.090
Or it could be somewhere on
premise with low bandwidth

00:22:53.090 --> 00:22:54.200
or no bandwidth at all.

00:22:54.200 --> 00:22:55.850
You have to see.

00:22:55.850 --> 00:22:58.400
So what do you do with
these type of situations?

00:22:58.400 --> 00:23:00.620
If you were to look at
it from that perspective,

00:23:00.620 --> 00:23:02.740
we have the Google
Transfer Service that's

00:23:02.740 --> 00:23:04.210
part of the Google Console.

00:23:04.210 --> 00:23:06.520
So you can go to storage,
and you can set up

00:23:06.520 --> 00:23:08.440
the Google Transfer Service.

00:23:08.440 --> 00:23:10.599
You can do it run
once, run daily,

00:23:10.599 --> 00:23:12.640
so you can think of it,
if you want to, as a cron

00:23:12.640 --> 00:23:15.910
job that you can do
to backup continuously

00:23:15.910 --> 00:23:16.930
as part of the process.

00:23:16.930 --> 00:23:19.510
And there is no limit
on the amount of data

00:23:19.510 --> 00:23:21.890
that you can do with the
Google Transfer Service.

00:23:21.890 --> 00:23:23.930
It can do retries as well.

00:23:23.930 --> 00:23:27.050
And it can help you
with that perspective.

00:23:27.050 --> 00:23:30.770
If you were to look at the colo
type of situation or on-prem

00:23:30.770 --> 00:23:32.920
with good connectivity,
then one of the primary

00:23:32.920 --> 00:23:34.390
answers we have is gsutil.

00:23:34.390 --> 00:23:36.980
You get gsutil as
part of the Cloud SDK.

00:23:36.980 --> 00:23:39.820
You can install it, and
you can start gsutil CP.

00:23:39.820 --> 00:23:41.680
And you can copy from
bucket to bucket,

00:23:41.680 --> 00:23:44.260
or you can copy from
one location to another.

00:23:44.260 --> 00:23:48.070
And it has the ability to shunt
the file into multiple ones,

00:23:48.070 --> 00:23:50.037
and aggregates them on the end.

00:23:50.037 --> 00:23:51.370
And it does all of that for you.

00:23:51.370 --> 00:23:53.770
It has retries,
can do all of that.

00:23:53.770 --> 00:23:56.740
If you don't like gsutil,
you have different vendors

00:23:56.740 --> 00:23:58.480
that you can use also.

00:23:58.480 --> 00:24:02.200
If you want an approach that a
vendor could come in and do it

00:24:02.200 --> 00:24:04.260
for you, you can use
BitSpeed, Aspera, Tervela,

00:24:04.260 --> 00:24:06.314
and there are other
vendors as well.

00:24:06.314 --> 00:24:08.230
I put the link later
where you can go and look

00:24:08.230 --> 00:24:10.642
at the latest and greatest.

00:24:10.642 --> 00:24:12.100
Then the third
option, if you don't

00:24:12.100 --> 00:24:15.710
have enough bandwidth
or no bandwidth at all,

00:24:15.710 --> 00:24:17.980
so if the data size is
less than 100 terabytes,

00:24:17.980 --> 00:24:21.504
usually you would get into the
Iron Mountain in North America.

00:24:21.504 --> 00:24:23.170
And that is the prime
focus technologies

00:24:23.170 --> 00:24:25.720
in EMEA and AsiaPac.

00:24:25.720 --> 00:24:28.690
And you could still use the
Google Transfer Appliance.

00:24:28.690 --> 00:24:30.430
So these are things
that you can do.

00:24:30.430 --> 00:24:32.860
If the data size goes
above 100 terabytes,

00:24:32.860 --> 00:24:35.380
you can have the Google
Transfer Appliance where they

00:24:35.380 --> 00:24:36.850
ship that appliance to you.

00:24:36.850 --> 00:24:39.130
You put the data, and then
ship it back to Google.

00:24:39.130 --> 00:24:41.450
And they'll import it back
to you, the right project

00:24:41.450 --> 00:24:44.680
to the right buckets in
Google Cloud Storage.

00:24:44.680 --> 00:24:46.040
And the same thing for Zadara.

00:24:46.040 --> 00:24:48.950
Zadara also has an
option for data hydration

00:24:48.950 --> 00:24:50.440
where it ships two devices.

00:24:50.440 --> 00:24:52.607
One you can put the
data and ship it back.

00:24:52.607 --> 00:24:54.190
The other one you
can keep it on prem,

00:24:54.190 --> 00:24:58.330
where it starts syncing the
data for delta type of changes

00:24:58.330 --> 00:25:00.720
down the road.

00:25:00.720 --> 00:25:03.750
Now this is pretty much
for the first entry

00:25:03.750 --> 00:25:08.280
into Google Cloud practice,
Google Cloud Storage.

00:25:08.280 --> 00:25:10.410
You may have structured data.

00:25:10.410 --> 00:25:12.720
And structured data is also--

00:25:12.720 --> 00:25:15.900
you can move it from
different types of sources

00:25:15.900 --> 00:25:17.850
to different type of things.

00:25:17.850 --> 00:25:19.387
And primarily for
structured data,

00:25:19.387 --> 00:25:20.970
if you looked at the
previous diagram,

00:25:20.970 --> 00:25:23.790
we have two databases
or two constructs.

00:25:23.790 --> 00:25:27.630
One is the cloud SQL
with MySQL and Postgres.

00:25:27.630 --> 00:25:30.540
And the other one is Spanner.

00:25:30.540 --> 00:25:34.380
You can, if you want to, use
the old, traditional ways

00:25:34.380 --> 00:25:40.640
of doing data dumps, compress
or dumps from SQL 1 to SQL 2.

00:25:40.640 --> 00:25:41.660
And you can do that.

00:25:41.660 --> 00:25:43.660
But lots of customers
don't want to do that.

00:25:43.660 --> 00:25:46.500
They want to migrate
or move tons of data,

00:25:46.500 --> 00:25:48.420
and they'd rather use a partner.

00:25:48.420 --> 00:25:49.860
You can do that, too.

00:25:49.860 --> 00:25:52.200
And you can do the same
thing for BigQuery.

00:25:52.200 --> 00:25:53.910
It's also a partner
play, where you

00:25:53.910 --> 00:25:56.430
can have different
type of sources coming

00:25:56.430 --> 00:26:00.090
from databases, SaaS data
warehouses, or B2B EDI

00:26:00.090 --> 00:26:00.720
type of stuff.

00:26:00.720 --> 00:26:03.060
And you have all these
partners that are basically

00:26:03.060 --> 00:26:06.780
having their different type of
solutions catering to migrating

00:26:06.780 --> 00:26:09.510
that data to BigQuery.

00:26:09.510 --> 00:26:12.690
And also, we have this that was
announced last year, that you

00:26:12.690 --> 00:26:17.430
can migrate and the ability to
ingest data from DoubleClick,

00:26:17.430 --> 00:26:19.740
the digital marketing
type of platform

00:26:19.740 --> 00:26:22.830
from Google, along with
AdWords and YouTube.

00:26:22.830 --> 00:26:24.510
And you have
additional sources that

00:26:24.510 --> 00:26:27.010
are listed under that link
for Facebook, Twitter,

00:26:27.010 --> 00:26:30.350
et cetera that can be part of
that process of migrating data

00:26:30.350 --> 00:26:31.740
to BigQuery.

00:26:31.740 --> 00:26:33.699
There are other people
who are on the other end

00:26:33.699 --> 00:26:35.948
of the spectrum, where they
have the right engineering

00:26:35.948 --> 00:26:36.810
or whatever it is.

00:26:36.810 --> 00:26:38.880
They would like to
tinker with their hands.

00:26:38.880 --> 00:26:41.290
And Google gives you
the ability to do that.

00:26:41.290 --> 00:26:46.960
So you can use gsutil tool
do that if you want to.

00:26:46.960 --> 00:26:49.150
So you can get the
data through gsutil

00:26:49.150 --> 00:26:50.490
to a Google Cloud Storage.

00:26:50.490 --> 00:26:54.270
And then you can use either
the bq command, if you want to,

00:26:54.270 --> 00:26:55.830
or you can use
the web UI, or you

00:26:55.830 --> 00:26:59.730
can use BigQuery APIs to
get the data into BigQuery.

00:26:59.730 --> 00:27:04.770
It's just a more manual
approach that you would

00:27:04.770 --> 00:27:08.340
have to be kind of doing that.

00:27:08.340 --> 00:27:10.030
Just something to keep in mind--

00:27:10.030 --> 00:27:13.650
bq tool, when you get
the data to BigQuery,

00:27:13.650 --> 00:27:15.810
you're basically making a
fundamental assumption--

00:27:15.810 --> 00:27:19.230
you are not enriching
the data or there's

00:27:19.230 --> 00:27:20.850
very minimal type
of changes that you

00:27:20.850 --> 00:27:22.470
need to do on the data.

00:27:22.470 --> 00:27:25.320
The moment you start saying,
I want to do multiple joins,

00:27:25.320 --> 00:27:27.255
I want to do some
data enrichment,

00:27:27.255 --> 00:27:29.380
then that's where data flow
comes into the picture.

00:27:29.380 --> 00:27:30.850
And I'll talk
about that shortly.

00:27:30.850 --> 00:27:34.290
But that basically is what this
fundamental premise here-- you

00:27:34.290 --> 00:27:36.990
can use user-defined
functions in BigQuery

00:27:36.990 --> 00:27:38.460
to do certain manipulations.

00:27:38.460 --> 00:27:41.310
But still, it's limited
in terms of the enrichment

00:27:41.310 --> 00:27:47.544
that you can do on the data set
before moving it to BigQuery.

00:27:47.544 --> 00:27:49.980
Now this slide--

00:27:49.980 --> 00:27:52.350
I don't recommend that
you show that slide

00:27:52.350 --> 00:27:58.680
to your customers or anybody
outside of being a techie,

00:27:58.680 --> 00:28:01.340
because it's overwhelming,
to be honest with you.

00:28:01.340 --> 00:28:02.970
If you, to show that,
hey, by the way,

00:28:02.970 --> 00:28:05.310
I'm going here to sell
you something on Google.

00:28:05.310 --> 00:28:07.710
And here's what Google
offers out of the table.

00:28:07.710 --> 00:28:10.050
It's complete opposite purpose.

00:28:10.050 --> 00:28:11.940
I just show it
