00:30:37.470 --> 00:30:41.100
and that does the job for you.

00:30:41.100 --> 00:30:45.380
So let's talk about path
to machine learning and AI.

00:30:45.380 --> 00:30:49.000
So machine learning
and the use cases,

00:30:49.000 --> 00:30:52.340
I doubt there has been
any session today where

00:30:52.340 --> 00:30:54.550
machine learning probably
was not mentioned somewhere

00:30:54.550 --> 00:30:55.730
in that session.

00:30:55.730 --> 00:30:58.192
We are at that level
of hype cycle that--

00:30:58.192 --> 00:30:59.150
and don't get me wrong.

00:30:59.150 --> 00:31:00.150
I love machine learning.

00:31:00.150 --> 00:31:02.090
I mean, I think it's a
wonderful technology.

00:31:02.090 --> 00:31:06.170
But with everything else
like we have done in IT, when

00:31:06.170 --> 00:31:08.140
we start looking at
something, and overhype it,

00:31:08.140 --> 00:31:10.390
and we start looking it's
the answer to every question

00:31:10.390 --> 00:31:13.550
that we have, that's where
the problems start arising.

00:31:13.550 --> 00:31:17.030
And I think we are at that level
where everybody says, and comes

00:31:17.030 --> 00:31:21.050
talk to us, I want to apply
machine learning to my X, Y, Z.

00:31:21.050 --> 00:31:24.260
And I bet you that many of
those type of customers,

00:31:24.260 --> 00:31:26.510
they don't have
the right awareness

00:31:26.510 --> 00:31:29.450
on what is meant by
machine learning,

00:31:29.450 --> 00:31:34.910
and if that is the right
set of tools or processes

00:31:34.910 --> 00:31:37.070
that would allow them
to address the problem.

00:31:37.070 --> 00:31:40.850
But as you can see, there
are lots of use cases

00:31:40.850 --> 00:31:44.240
that are listed here across
different type of industries,

00:31:44.240 --> 00:31:46.850
that basically
everybody is looking

00:31:46.850 --> 00:31:49.850
at saying, predictive
maintenance or condition

00:31:49.850 --> 00:31:50.630
monitoring.

00:31:50.630 --> 00:31:54.140
We've done that with
predictive model in the past.

00:31:54.140 --> 00:31:57.391
Things were OK, probably
80%, 90% accuracy.

00:31:57.391 --> 00:31:59.390
Now with machine learning,
additional constructs

00:31:59.390 --> 00:32:03.080
that are happening, you can get
to higher levels of accuracy.

00:32:03.080 --> 00:32:05.900
So Google basically
says, we have

00:32:05.900 --> 00:32:08.749
elements of machine learning
in every product that we have.

00:32:08.749 --> 00:32:09.290
That's great.

00:32:09.290 --> 00:32:10.520
That's Google.

00:32:10.520 --> 00:32:13.580
But the majority of customers
out there, they're not Google.

00:32:13.580 --> 00:32:16.010
So you have to be
very careful when

00:32:16.010 --> 00:32:18.020
it comes to machine
learning, and say,

00:32:18.020 --> 00:32:21.060
am I addressing the
right set of questions?

00:32:21.060 --> 00:32:23.180
Am I having the
right to use cases

00:32:23.180 --> 00:32:24.627
as part of machine learning?

00:32:24.627 --> 00:32:26.210
And is it the right
thing for me to do

00:32:26.210 --> 00:32:29.010
as part of the organization?

00:32:29.010 --> 00:32:33.170
And if you look at
transforming data into actions,

00:32:33.170 --> 00:32:35.840
Google provides different
type of concerns

00:32:35.840 --> 00:32:38.090
when it comes from data
ingestion all the way down

00:32:38.090 --> 00:32:41.870
to advanced analytics, and
machine learning, and abilities

00:32:41.870 --> 00:32:44.060
to do that.

00:32:44.060 --> 00:32:46.580
And these type of steps
would be required.

00:32:46.580 --> 00:32:51.415
If you attended the Miles
Ward session, the first thing

00:32:51.415 --> 00:32:52.790
that we do in
machine learning, I

00:32:52.790 --> 00:32:54.980
have to get the data into GCP.

00:32:54.980 --> 00:32:56.600
Somewhere, I need
to get the data,

00:32:56.600 --> 00:32:58.292
and then submitting
applications.

00:32:58.292 --> 00:32:59.750
And that generates
additional data.

00:32:59.750 --> 00:33:01.850
Then you start applying
machine learning.

00:33:01.850 --> 00:33:02.660
So these are key.

00:33:02.660 --> 00:33:07.820
And that's part of the ecosystem
that Google provides in order

00:33:07.820 --> 00:33:11.510
to give you that ability to
ingest the data, all the way

00:33:11.510 --> 00:33:14.120
up to creating that advanced
analytics and intelligence,

00:33:14.120 --> 00:33:16.070
and apply machine
learning onto it.

00:33:16.070 --> 00:33:18.770
And if you are interested
in what type of constructs

00:33:18.770 --> 00:33:21.850
that you are looking
into, here's the diagram.

00:33:21.850 --> 00:33:24.560
Again I wouldn't go
and talk to my customer

00:33:24.560 --> 00:33:27.230
and say, here, look how
Google is great, and all that.

00:33:27.230 --> 00:33:31.140
Because that basically
tells complexity.

00:33:31.140 --> 00:33:32.990
So we want to be more
prescriptive as part

00:33:32.990 --> 00:33:35.480
of the process, and figure
out what type of things

00:33:35.480 --> 00:33:37.520
that we need to
do in order to get

00:33:37.520 --> 00:33:43.470
to that level of complexity.

00:33:43.470 --> 00:33:50.270
So if you were to get
the data into GCP,

00:33:50.270 --> 00:33:53.870
here are some of the things
usually that we see as big data

00:33:53.870 --> 00:33:54.980
reference architecture.

00:33:54.980 --> 00:33:58.310
So typical components, these
are the typical suspects.

00:33:58.310 --> 00:34:00.620
I have two types of workloads.

00:34:00.620 --> 00:34:02.990
One could be potentially
streaming workload.

00:34:02.990 --> 00:34:05.030
The other one is
a batch workload.

00:34:05.030 --> 00:34:08.630
And the objective is to get
it somewhere into BigQuery

00:34:08.630 --> 00:34:11.300
or Bigtable, or whatever it is.

00:34:11.300 --> 00:34:14.100
And usually, we look
into it, and say,

00:34:14.100 --> 00:34:17.090
for Pub/Sub type
of streaming, we

00:34:17.090 --> 00:34:19.639
have a Cloud Pub/Sub that
we want to incorporate

00:34:19.639 --> 00:34:22.280
as part of the architecture.

00:34:22.280 --> 00:34:26.239
And then, I could
take that stream,

00:34:26.239 --> 00:34:28.489
and let it feed
through data flow,

00:34:28.489 --> 00:34:30.280
because I want to do
certain manipulations.

00:34:30.280 --> 00:34:33.800
I could do certain enrichments
on it, whatever I want to do.

00:34:33.800 --> 00:34:37.429
And then I can have a
resting place in BigQuery.

00:34:37.429 --> 00:34:40.850
So that would be one
path to look into it.

00:34:40.850 --> 00:34:42.710
You can take a batch load.

00:34:42.710 --> 00:34:46.370
You can put that batch load in
Google Cloud Storage, the GCS

00:34:46.370 --> 00:34:47.659
component.

00:34:47.659 --> 00:34:49.310
And you can take
that batch load,

00:34:49.310 --> 00:34:52.670
and say, I want to apply
certain enrichments on it

00:34:52.670 --> 00:34:54.210
as part of the process.

00:34:54.210 --> 00:34:56.719
And then for this
particular load,

00:34:56.719 --> 00:34:59.600
the data could be more
appropriate for being stored

00:34:59.600 --> 00:35:03.380
as part of Google Bigtable,
because I am very concerned

00:35:03.380 --> 00:35:05.570
about latency, and
Bigtable gives you

00:35:05.570 --> 00:35:08.960
that type of access to it.

00:35:08.960 --> 00:35:13.790
Or you can say, I would like
to apply certain Dataproc type

00:35:13.790 --> 00:35:16.670
of capabilities,
manipulations, and then store

00:35:16.670 --> 00:35:18.980
the data in Cloud Bigtable.

00:35:18.980 --> 00:35:20.690
You still have
the option, if you

00:35:20.690 --> 00:35:24.680
wanted to store data
in cloud Dataproc,

00:35:24.680 --> 00:35:30.590
you can use Dataproc HDFS
on SSD or persistent disks.

00:35:30.590 --> 00:35:32.780
But that's not necessarily
a good approach,

00:35:32.780 --> 00:35:36.560
because it's more expensive to
store that data in Dataproc.

00:35:36.560 --> 00:35:40.870
The best practice is to
have the data stored in GCS,

00:35:40.870 --> 00:35:44.920
and allow Dataproc to run
clusters that would, basically,

00:35:44.920 --> 00:35:46.960
figure out what would
be the right destination

00:35:46.960 --> 00:35:48.170
for that data.

00:35:48.170 --> 00:35:51.160
So this is pretty much
the end-to-end way

00:35:51.160 --> 00:35:54.070
of looking at a big data
reference architecture,

00:35:54.070 --> 00:35:56.920
by allowing you to get a
streaming load, or a batch

00:35:56.920 --> 00:35:59.890
workload, to get
the data into GCP.

00:35:59.890 --> 00:36:02.350
And then you have,
on the far right end,

00:36:02.350 --> 00:36:06.010
the ability for any type
of data for the data

00:36:06.010 --> 00:36:10.600
exploration, machine learning
type of activities, analytics,

00:36:10.600 --> 00:36:13.270
dashboards, whatever it is
that you can start looking

00:36:13.270 --> 00:36:15.970
and applying on these
type of data sets

00:36:15.970 --> 00:36:19.720
that you have stored
as part of BigQuery.

00:36:19.720 --> 00:36:26.050
Now let's dissect that piece by
piece, and look at it, and say,

00:36:26.050 --> 00:36:27.859
I have a streaming load.

00:36:27.859 --> 00:36:29.650
I just want to get it
directly to BigQuery.

00:36:29.650 --> 00:36:30.340
Can I do that?

00:36:30.340 --> 00:36:32.207
The answer is yes, you can.

00:36:32.207 --> 00:36:34.540
There's nothing that says you
can't, with the assumption

00:36:34.540 --> 00:36:36.430
that you're not
manipulating the data.

00:36:36.430 --> 00:36:37.850
We talked about that earlier.

00:36:37.850 --> 00:36:40.840
So you can get the streaming
workload or a batch workload,

00:36:40.840 --> 00:36:42.820
and put it directly
in for BigQuery.

00:36:42.820 --> 00:36:45.160
And you can do
simple manipulations

00:36:45.160 --> 00:36:49.600
on the bq command, and data
will get into BigQuery.

00:36:49.600 --> 00:36:53.680
If you want to go and say,
I have a batch workload.

00:36:53.680 --> 00:36:57.400
I would like to go and put that
into my Google Cloud Storage.

00:36:57.400 --> 00:37:00.160
I would like to do
some type of enrichment

00:37:00.160 --> 00:37:02.650
on that type of
workload, and then

00:37:02.650 --> 00:37:06.010
I want to batch that
pipeline into BigQuery,

00:37:06.010 --> 00:37:09.820
and that's also an option
that's available to you.

00:37:09.820 --> 00:37:14.340
And then this one is, I
have an on-prem type of data

00:37:14.340 --> 00:37:18.030
that I parked on prem
through an ETL job.

00:37:18.030 --> 00:37:19.410
Now I need to move it--

00:37:19.410 --> 00:37:24.420
it's a relational, so I need
to move to Cloud SQL, into GCP.

00:37:24.420 --> 00:37:27.990
And then I take that
data from Cloud SQL,

00:37:27.990 --> 00:37:30.690
and I can run it
through my data pipeline

00:37:30.690 --> 00:37:32.790
again against the
cloud data flow,

00:37:32.790 --> 00:37:34.470
do some additional
enrichments, joins,

00:37:34.470 --> 00:37:36.570
or whatever it is that
you want to do with it,

00:37:36.570 --> 00:37:38.930
and then feed it into BigQuery.

00:37:38.930 --> 00:37:42.090
That's also a pattern
that we see out there.

00:37:42.090 --> 00:37:46.830
Or you can have a fan-in-fan-out
type of multiple streaming type

00:37:46.830 --> 00:37:51.480
of clients, that you
want to get the data.

00:37:51.480 --> 00:37:53.730
And as part of
getting that data,

00:37:53.730 --> 00:37:57.060
you want to do different type
of processing for that data.

00:37:57.060 --> 00:37:59.910
And you have different
instances of BigQuery

00:37:59.910 --> 00:38:03.390
where you have
Instance A of BigQuery

00:38:03.390 --> 00:38:04.950
and instance B of BigQuery.

00:38:04.950 --> 00:38:08.010
And you can have the
right job being streamed

00:38:08.010 --> 00:38:11.280
to the right channel
that becomes the ending

00:38:11.280 --> 00:38:13.530
resting place for BigQuery.

00:38:13.530 --> 00:38:15.570
And you can have,
also, additional type

00:38:15.570 --> 00:38:18.330
of manipulations, where
you can stream and compute

00:38:18.330 --> 00:38:19.190
before you publish.

00:38:19.190 --> 00:38:21.870
So I stream my data
from a financial type

00:38:21.870 --> 00:38:25.050
of company or organization.

00:38:25.050 --> 00:38:28.200
And as part of the process the
data, gets into Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:38:28.200 --> 00:38:29.880
And then I do
certain manipulations

00:38:29.880 --> 00:38:32.550
on the data, certain
enrichments, calculations.

00:38:32.550 --> 00:38:35.070
And then I add additional
type of windowing,

00:38:35.070 --> 00:38:37.710
or do time type of
series calculations.

00:38:37.710 --> 00:38:41.460
And then I calculate certain
correlations, and then

00:38:41.460 --> 00:38:43.680
feed that again to a
Cloud Pub/Sub, where

00:38:43.680 --> 00:38:46.692
I have multiple subscribers,
where I'm trying to figure out

00:38:46.692 --> 00:38:48.150
how the stock market
is going to do

00:38:48.150 --> 00:38:50.790
or how certain stocks
are going to behave

00:38:50.790 --> 00:38:54.150
under certain types of
situation or conditions.

00:38:54.150 --> 00:38:56.340
And I can feed that
into a trading system

00:38:56.340 --> 00:38:59.370
where there could be people
who are waiting or acting

00:38:59.370 --> 00:39:01.470
on these type of intelligence.

00:39:01.470 --> 00:39:04.680
Or I could feed that
back into BigQuery,

00:39:04.680 --> 00:39:07.710
where I can apply certain
dashboarding analytics, machine

00:39:07.710 --> 00:39:10.020
learning, whatever it
is that I want to do.

00:39:10.020 --> 00:39:13.050
And I can start exploring
with Cloud Datalab as part

00:39:13.050 --> 00:39:14.200
of the process.

00:39:14.200 --> 00:39:17.150
So what this is
trying to show you,

00:39:17.150 --> 00:39:20.880
the suspects that you have
seen, they're very common.

00:39:20.880 --> 00:39:26.640
So we talk about Pub/Sub,
Dataflow, BigQuery, Bigtable,

00:39:26.640 --> 00:39:30.630
and Datalab as part
of the top components

00:39:30.630 --> 00:39:33.300
that are usually involved,
and GCS, obviously.

00:39:33.300 --> 00:39:37.440
These are the
primary ones that are

00:39:37.440 --> 00:39:43.240
involved as part of the process
of getting data into GCP.

00:39:43.240 --> 00:39:45.730
And here's an example--

00:39:45.730 --> 00:39:48.340
interesting.

00:39:48.340 --> 00:39:50.650
So here's an example
where it basically

00:39:50.650 --> 00:39:56.790
shows I have an existing data
lake that's installed on prem.

00:39:56.790 --> 00:40:01.110
And as part of that process,
I have different type

00:40:01.110 --> 00:40:04.480
of data sources.

00:40:04.480 --> 00:40:08.910
And I need to feed that
data into Google type.

00:40:08.910 --> 00:40:11.820
So I have different
type of constructs

00:40:11.820 --> 00:40:14.140
that I want to incorporate.

00:40:14.140 --> 00:40:16.860
So I have the GCS connector,
where basically, I

00:40:16.860 --> 00:40:22.470
can push the data
directly to GCS,

00:40:22.470 --> 00:40:25.800
because GCS is a
Hadoop-compliant type of file

00:40:25.800 --> 00:40:26.940
storage system.

00:40:26.940 --> 00:40:28.620
So using that GCS
connector, I can

00:40:28.620 --> 00:40:32.580
use the push model, where I can
push the data directly to GCS,

00:40:32.580 --> 00:40:37.410
or I can aggregate the data, do
some type of enrichment on it,

00:40:37.410 --> 00:40:39.240
and I can use a partner--

00:40:39.240 --> 00:40:42.520
happens to be Informatica or
anything, it doesn't matter.

00:40:42.520 --> 00:40:45.240
And I can use that to
push the data to GCP.

00:40:45.240 --> 00:40:50.085
And then I can feed that into
my pipeline of cloud data flow

00:40:50.085 --> 00:40:51.930
or cloud data prep,
which also allows

00:40:51.930 --> 00:40:54.030
you to do some
additional data profiling

00:40:54.030 --> 00:40:55.480
as part of the process.

00:40:55.480 --> 00:40:59.160
And then you can have the option
to move the data into Cleanse

00:40:59.160 --> 00:41:01.530
Data as part of the storage.

00:41:01.530 --> 00:41:04.350
And this is basically
ingesting into a data lake.

00:41:04.350 --> 00:41:06.720
And as I mentioned earlier
as part of the data lake,

00:41:06.720 --> 00:41:10.380
we try to avoid storing
stuff in Dataproc.

00:41:10.380 --> 00:41:15.900
So the preferred method is to
use the cheaper storage of GCS.

00:41:15.900 --> 00:41:19.680
And if your workload
happens to be more

00:41:19.680 --> 00:41:21.581
appropriate for Bigtable, sure.

00:41:21.581 --> 00:41:22.080
Why not?

00:41:22.080 --> 00:41:23.040
You can do that.

00:41:23.040 --> 00:41:24.810
And in many
instances, you'll see

00:41:24.810 --> 00:41:29.130
that people will go against
BigQuery to store the data,

00:41:29.130 --> 00:41:31.710
and use Dataproc-- ephemeral
clusters, basically--

00:41:31.710 --> 00:41:35.130
to run certain jobs, and store
the data in the right trusting

00:41:35.130 --> 00:41:35.770
location.

00:41:35.770 --> 00:41:37.414
And you can apply
the visualization

00:41:37.414 --> 00:41:38.705
on that as part of the process.

00:41:41.350 --> 00:41:44.170
And this gets us into the
machine learning lifecycle.

00:41:44.170 --> 00:41:46.450
So the machine
learning lifecycle--

00:41:46.450 --> 00:41:48.340
you know if you
attended Miles Ward,

00:41:48.340 --> 00:41:50.010
you probably have
seen this diagram.

00:41:50.010 --> 00:41:53.230
I did not have it, and I
recreated it for this session,

00:41:53.230 --> 00:41:55.570
so I'll give credit to that.

00:41:55.570 --> 00:41:58.240
So this basically
defines the first thing

00:41:58.240 --> 00:42:01.330
that you need to do as part
of any machine learning

00:42:01.330 --> 00:42:02.800
is to look at the use cases.

00:42:02.800 --> 00:42:05.050
And I alluded to that
earlier in the session.

00:42:05.050 --> 00:42:07.510
That becomes a really
important type of factor

00:42:07.510 --> 00:42:10.210
to make sure that you are
addressing the right ones,

00:42:10.210 --> 00:42:13.480
and defining the success
criteria that you want to do.

00:42:13.480 --> 00:42:14.930
Then we get into
data exploration.

00:42:14.930 --> 00:42:18.820
Basically, I want to do
exploratory data analysis.

00:42:18.820 --> 00:42:21.280
And you can use any type of
clustering type of mechanisms.

00:42:21.280 --> 00:42:23.650
I'm trying to reduce
the amount of parameters

00:42:23.650 --> 00:42:26.127
that I want to build
the model from.

00:42:26.127 --> 00:42:28.210
And I can go ahead and
select what type of machine

00:42:28.210 --> 00:42:31.540
learning algorithm, and I can
do, later, further feature

00:42:31.540 --> 00:42:33.520
engineering, which
basically looks

00:42:33.520 --> 00:42:37.720
into additional types of
criteria and selecting

00:42:37.720 --> 00:42:40.570
the right parameters
for the right model.

00:42:40.570 --> 00:42:43.060
And you build your
first iteration

00:42:43.060 --> 00:42:45.080
of the machine learning model.

00:42:45.080 --> 00:42:47.890
And you iterate
on that with kind

00:42:47.890 --> 00:42:52.510
of building your minimum
viable type of model.

00:42:52.510 --> 00:42:55.004
And once you do that, you
basically look at it and say,

00:42:55.004 --> 00:42:56.920
are there things that
we can change or improve

00:42:56.920 --> 00:42:58.730
as part of the process?

00:42:58.730 --> 00:43:02.590
And then you start presenting
results that, yeah, you told us

00:43:02.590 --> 00:43:06.580
that we can challenge
the current supply chain

00:43:06.580 --> 00:43:08.360
model that you have
in order to give you

00:43:08.360 --> 00:43:13.270
better accuracy and cheaper way
to get these data parts coming

00:43:13.270 --> 00:43:15.520
to the US through different
vendors and routes.

00:43:15.520 --> 00:43:18.216
And here's the model who
would allow us to do that.

00:43:18.216 --> 00:43:19.840
So let's look at
these results, and see

00:43:19.840 --> 00:43:22.360
if there is any value
to them, or if the older

00:43:22.360 --> 00:43:25.710
model is a better type
of performing model.

00:43:25.710 --> 00:43:27.460
And then you start
looking at and planning

00:43:27.460 --> 00:43:29.530
for deployment in production.

00:43:29.530 --> 00:43:33.190
And that's where you start
getting into interesting type

00:43:33.190 --> 00:43:34.047
of setups--

00:43:34.047 --> 00:43:36.130
so what type of production,
what type of clusters,

00:43:36.130 --> 00:43:37.810
what type of data ingestion?

00:43:37.810 --> 00:43:39.805
How busy are the
machines going to be?

00:43:39.805 --> 00:43:41.430
Are they going to be
busy all the time?

00:43:41.430 --> 00:43:43.000
Are you going to
stream the data?

00:43:43.000 --> 00:43:44.170
Is it batch, et cetera?

00:43:44.170 --> 00:43:47.020
Are you going to run
this at the same time

00:43:47.020 --> 00:43:48.550
while other data is coming?

00:43:48.550 --> 00:43:50.260
So all these are
type of questions.

00:43:50.260 --> 00:43:53.400
And usually what we do,
we start with the POC--

00:43:53.400 --> 00:43:57.070
a simple POC that doesn't
factor any of this into it.

00:43:57.070 --> 00:43:59.380
And people think that
once they've done the POC,

00:43:59.380 --> 00:44:01.474
a lot of the legwork
has been done.

00:44:01.474 --> 00:44:02.890
And you get into
the previous path

00:44:02.890 --> 00:44:04.431
that we talked about
with data lakes.

00:44:04.431 --> 00:44:05.170
It's not.

00:44:05.170 --> 00:44:06.730
There's a lot of
consideration that

00:44:06.730 --> 00:44:10.030
needs to be taken from 7
through 10 to make sure

00:44:10.030 --> 00:44:13.420
that we have the right
models, we monitor them, they,

00:44:13.420 --> 00:44:16.000
are the right ones
the most efficient.

00:44:16.000 --> 00:44:18.550
And sometimes there could
be some performance issues

00:44:18.550 --> 00:44:20.420
that could arise in the future.

00:44:20.420 --> 00:44:21.730
So what do you do about those?

00:44:21.730 --> 00:44:24.021
You go and rebuild the model,
and you repeat the cycle.

00:44:24.021 --> 00:44:26.110
So the whole process
is iterative.

00:44:26.110 --> 00:44:28.450
And lots of complexities
in the details here.

00:44:28.450 --> 00:44:30.970
It's not just building
a POC in six weeks,

00:44:30.970 --> 00:44:32.120
and we're done with that.

00:44:34.900 --> 00:44:36.962
And you've seen this,
probably, before if you've

00:44:36.962 --> 00:44:38.170
been to many of the sessions.

00:44:38.170 --> 00:44:41.200
You can use your own
pre-built, ready to use models.

00:44:41.200 --> 00:44:44.320
There is no point for you to
go and create a model that

00:44:44.320 --> 00:44:46.150
can go and recognize objects.

00:44:46.150 --> 00:44:47.650
We're talking to
different companies

00:44:47.650 --> 00:44:50.290
right now to build solutions
around these things.

00:44:50.290 --> 00:44:51.610
I can look at satellite images.

00:44:51.610 --> 00:44:54.370
I can look at third-party
data, weather data,

00:44:54.370 --> 00:44:57.182
and look at natural language
processing, Translate API,

00:44:57.182 --> 00:44:59.140
to come up with a solution
that would give them

00:44:59.140 --> 00:45:01.150
a better score when it
comes to underwriting

00:45:01.150 --> 00:45:03.850
processes for insurance.

00:45:03.850 --> 00:45:06.100
So these are things that
you can put together

00:45:06.100 --> 00:45:07.090
as part of the process.

00:45:07.090 --> 00:45:09.790
You can build a model
that takes elements

00:45:09.790 --> 00:45:13.750
of these types of factors,
and incorporate that

00:45:13.750 --> 00:45:16.840
into a model that can give
you a better result than what

00:45:16.840 --> 00:45:18.820
you may be having today.

00:45:18.820 --> 00:45:21.590
And you can use your own
data to train these models.

00:45:21.590 --> 00:45:25.780
You feel free to build your
own models using TensorFlow.

00:45:25.780 --> 00:45:28.480
And feel free to upload
them to Google Cloud.

00:45:28.480 --> 00:45:30.940
Let them run in the cloud,
and see what type of results

00:45:30.940 --> 00:45:35.050
that you can get out of that,
and empower your business

00:45:35.050 --> 00:45:37.870
to be the right business.

00:45:37.870 --> 00:45:41.620
I have one minute left I want
to spend on this client example.

00:45:41.620 --> 00:45:44.310
I talked mostly about what
this thing is all about.

00:45:44.310 --> 00:45:45.660
But this is a customer.

00:45:45.660 --> 00:45:48.220
The pattern that we're seeing--

00:45:48.220 --> 00:45:54.390
they were running
Cloudera on-prem,

00:45:54.390 --> 00:45:55.750
and spending a lot of money.

00:45:55.750 --> 00:45:58.300
Really, that was the
primary driver for them.

00:45:58.300 --> 00:46:00.970
They were basically spending
about $20K per month

00:46:00.970 --> 00:46:03.940
on their Cloudera type of setup.

00:46:03.940 --> 00:46:08.950
And they came to us and said, we
would like to move that to GCP.

00:46:08.950 --> 00:46:11.870
So if you look at
the architecture,

00:46:11.870 --> 00:46:13.850
this is what they
have as part of this.

00:46:13.850 --> 00:46:22.780
So they have Cloudera running
on GCP in VMs type of formats.

00:46:22.780 --> 00:46:25.690
And the solution was
basically to get them

00:46:25.690 --> 00:46:30.430
to the right set up using
Dataproc and ephemeral

00:46:30.430 --> 00:46:31.930
[INAUDIBLE],, and
using Google Cloud

00:46:31.930 --> 00:46:34.450
Storage as part of the process.

00:46:34.450 --> 00:46:36.760
The previous session I was
here in the last 10, 15

00:46:36.760 --> 00:46:39.400
minutes, the gentleman was
citing different statistics

00:46:39.400 --> 00:46:45.130
about moving the Hadoop
infrastructures into Dataproc.

00:46:45.130 --> 00:46:48.640
He was citing 70% savings
on hardware, 80% or more

00:46:48.640 --> 00:46:49.720
on software.

00:46:49.720 --> 00:46:52.240
We saved this company
from $20,000 down

00:46:52.240 --> 00:46:54.080
to $4,000 per month.

00:46:54.080 --> 00:46:55.510
And its about 80% savings.

00:46:55.510 --> 00:46:58.240
So I'm glad we have
the consistency

00:46:58.240 --> 00:47:01.210
in these type of settings
as part of the savings

00:47:01.210 --> 00:47:04.090
that you can get from
migrating these existing Hadoop

00:47:04.090 --> 00:47:10.750
workloads from on-prem,
or using VMs on GCP.

00:47:10.750 --> 00:47:13.720
Sometimes, people think I'm
using VM on GCP for Cloudera,

00:47:13.720 --> 00:47:14.800
I'm still on the cloud.

00:47:14.800 --> 00:47:15.450
No, you're not.

00:47:15.450 --> 00:47:18.370
Well, you are, but it's not the
most efficient way to do it.

00:47:18.370 --> 00:47:19.990
There are better ways to do it.

00:47:19.990 --> 00:47:22.180
And this is one of those
things that you can

00:47:22.180 --> 00:47:24.160
use as part of that process.

00:47:24.160 --> 00:47:27.210
[MUSIC PLAYING]

